# GitHub Actions CI Rules

## Intent
Define mandatory CI enforcement using GitHub Actions for the rewrite product.

## Workflow Index (read this first)
For a plain-language list of every workflow — its name, when it runs, and what it does —
see [`.github/workflows/README.md`](../workflows/README.md). It also explains how the
"Build and push Docker images" and "Render deploy" workflows relate. Keep that index in
sync whenever you add, remove, or rename a workflow.

## Mandatory (MVP Exemption)
**NOTE: During MVP only (temporary), automated test gates and contract validation are disabled per product decision. Lint and type-check remain required. These gates will be restored for post-MVP hardening.**

- All merge and release gates must run through GitHub Actions workflows.
- Protect default branch with required status checks from GitHub Actions.
- Require passing checks for lint and type-check (MVP: unit tests, integration tests, and security/compliance gates DISABLED).
- ~~Run contract validation for plugin command, access-policy, and audit schemas.~~ [DISABLED FOR MVP]
- Block deployment workflows when any required compliance check fails.
- ~~Enforce build/test order: `shared` gates pass before `web` and `mobile` gates.~~ [DISABLED FOR MVP]
- Require Android release workflow to publish a signed APK to GitHub Releases.
- Require Expo EAS preview builds for mobile pull requests that affect mobile code.
- Require controlled EAS Update channel promotion for JavaScript/asset-only mobile updates.

## Workflow Naming Convention (Critical — all agents)
Every workflow's display `name:` (the top-of-file `name:` field that shows in the Actions tab and
the manual "Run workflow" menu) must read **service-or-category first, then what it does**, joined by
a spaced em dash:

```
name: <Service or category> — <What it does>
```

- **Service/category comes first.** Lead with the system the workflow acts on or the family it
  belongs to — `Expo`, `Neon`, `Formance`, `Render`, `Bug Reports`, `Cleanup`, `Generate`,
  `Inspect`, `GitHub Actions`, `GitHub Pages`. This groups related workflows together when the list
  is sorted alphabetically, so a human scanning the Actions tab can find or run the right one fast.
- **Separator is a spaced em dash** (` — `, the `—` character with one space on each side), not a
  hyphen, colon, or slash.
- **Right side says what it does**, in Title Case, short and plain — `Android Release`,
  `Create Branch`, `Nightly Backup to Private Repo`, `Deprecated Workflow Runs`.
- This governs the human-readable `name:` only. It does **not** rename the workflow **file** or any
  `job`/`step` id — those stay as they are unless a task explicitly asks to rename them.

Examples (current workflows):

```
name: Expo — Android Release
name: Expo — OTA Update
name: Neon — Create Branch
name: Formance — Nightly Backup to Private Repo
name: Cleanup — Deprecated Workflow Runs
name: Bug Reports — Create Github Issues
name: GitHub Actions — Budget Monitor
name: GitHub Pages — Deploy Blog
```

When you **create** a new workflow or **rename** an existing one, set its `name:` in this shape, and
update the Workflow Index ([`.github/workflows/README.md`](../workflows/README.md)) to match.

## Scheduled Workflows — Cron Staggering (Critical — all agents)
Whenever you add a `schedule:` cron, or edit an existing one, **stagger it against the schedules
already in the repo.** GitHub delays scheduled runs that land on a busy minute (especially the top of
the hour) when the platform is under load, so two jobs set to the same minute can both slip — and they
also compete for the same runner capacity. The repo convention is to spread schedules out.

Before you set a cron time, do this:

1. **List the existing schedules.** Search every workflow for its cron lines, e.g.
   `if command -v rg >/dev/null 2>&1; then rg -n "cron:" .github/workflows; else grep -RIn "cron:" .github/workflows; fi`.
   Expand each one mentally to the actual UTC times it fires (e.g. `0 */8 * * *` → 00:00, 08:00,
   16:00; `7,27,47 * * * *` → every hour at :07/:27/:47).
2. **Pick an unused, off-the-hour minute.** Never use minute `:00`. Avoid the minutes the frequent
   hourly/every-few-hours jobs already occupy (today: `:07`, `:15`, `:17`, `:23`, `:27`, `:30`, `:47`).
   Choose an odd minute that nothing else uses at the hour(s) your job runs, so your run has its own
   slot.
3. **Avoid hour collisions too.** If several daily jobs already fire in a given hour, move yours to a
   quieter hour or clearly off their minute — do not pile onto an hour that is already crowded.
4. **Write the chosen time and the reason in a comment** next to the `cron:` line (the actual UTC
   times it fires and a short note that it is offset to avoid a same-minute stampede), matching the
   existing cron comments.

This applies to every agent and every scheduled workflow — new or edited. A cron that is not staggered
against the existing schedules is a defect, the same as a failing check.

## Workflow Rules
- Separate workflows for pull requests, main branch, and release promotion.
- Use least-privilege GitHub tokens and scoped secrets.
- Store secrets only in GitHub Actions encrypted secrets/environments.
- Require manual approval steps for production environments.
- Cache package manager store and Android/Gradle dependencies for stable performance.

## Artifact Rules
- Upload test, scan, and policy-validation artifacts for traceability.
- Keep CI evidence retention aligned with compliance evidence policies.

## Prohibited
- Skipping required checks via admin override except documented emergency process.
- Running production deploy from local machine-only workflows.